Firefox is available. I can do a headless smoke test with Firefox: load the page, capture console errors, and take a screenshot. Firefox headless: `firefox --headless --screenshot /path.png --window-size=1280,800 file:///...` — but ES modules over file:// are blocked by CORS in Firefox too. I need to serve over HTTP. I can start a tiny static server with python3 or node, then run firefox headless screenshot.

Also, WebGL in headless Firefox should work (software GL via SwiftShader maybe). Let's try.

Plan:
1. Start a static server in background (python3 -m http.server or a small node server).
2. Run `firefox --headless --screenshot shot.png --window-size=1280,800 http://localhost:8000/index.html` — wait a bit for load; firefox screenshot waits for load event, but our animation runs after; screenshot timing may capture early frame but that's fine — we mainly want no errors and a rendered image.
3. Also capture console messages? Firefox headless screenshot doesn't show console. I could add `--jsconsole`? Not in headless. Alternative: use Marionette? Too complex.

Better approach for error checking: create a test page that imports main.js and writes errors to document.title or into the DOM, then screenshot shows it. Or simpler: use Firefox headless with a small wrapper page that catches window.onerror and renders the error text into the page, then screenshot.

Actually simplest: modify nothing; create `test.html` that does:
```html
<script type="module">
window.addEventListener('error', e => {
  document.body.textContent = 'ERROR: ' + e.message + ' @ ' + e.filename + ':' + e.lineno;
  document.body.style.color = 'red';
});
import('./main.js').catch(err => {
  document.body.textContent = 'IMPORT ERROR: ' + err.message;
  document.body.style.color = 'red';
});
</script>
```
Then screenshot test.html. If the pelican renders, the screenshot will show the scene; if there's an error, red text.

